# React and application UI

React 19. Function components only. No class components, no `forwardRef`
ceremony — in React 19 `ref` is an ordinary prop.

## The shared kit is not optional

Applications compose `@slate/ui-kit`; they do not build their own primitives.
If an app needs a button, a dialog, a menu, or a scroll area, it imports one.
If the kit lacks something, add it to the kit — that is what makes three apps
look like one product.

An app may define components, but only ones that are genuinely specific to it
(`app-grid.component.tsx` in the Launcher). The moment a second app wants it,
it moves to the kit.

## Component structure

```tsx
// button.component.tsx
type ButtonProps = {
  variant?: ButtonVariant;
  size?: ButtonSize;
} & React.ComponentPropsWithRef<'button'>;

export function Button({ variant = 'default', size = 'md', className, ...props }: ButtonProps) {
  return <button className={cn(buttonVariants({ variant, size }), className)} {...props} />;
}
```

- Props type declared immediately above the component, named `<Component>Props`.
- Variants live in a sibling `*.variants.ts` using `cva`, never as branching
  inside the component body.
- Every component spreads the rest of the native props and merges `className`
  through `cn()`. A component that swallows `className` cannot be composed.
- One exported component per file.

## State

| Scope                        | Use                                            |
| ---------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------- |
| One component                | `useState` / `useReducer`                      |
| A subtree                    | Context, created in `app/app.providers.tsx`    |
| Whole application            | A Zustand store in `state/*.store.ts`          |
| Owned by the Rust backend    | An IPC command plus an event subscription      |

Never mirror backend state into a store "for convenience" — that creates two
sources of truth that drift. Read it through `@slate/ipc` and subscribe to the
event that invalidates it.

Zustand stores expose actions, not setters:

```ts
// good
useWindowStore.getState().maximize();
// bad
useWindowStore.setState({ isMaximized: true });
```

## Effects

`useEffect` is for synchronising with something outside React — a Tauri event
listener, a media query, a resize observer. It is not for deriving state.
Derived values are computed during render.

Every effect that subscribes must return a cleanup that unsubscribes. A Tauri
`listen()` returns an unlisten function; dropping it leaks a listener for the
lifetime of the window.

## Accessibility

Not negotiable, and largely free because Radix does the work:

- Interactive elements are real `<button>`/`<a>` elements, or Radix primitives.
- Every icon-only control has an accessible name.
- Focus is always visible; the focus ring is a design token, never removed.
- Dialogs and menus trap focus and restore it on close — use Radix, do not
  hand-roll this.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`; the kit's motion tokens already do.

## Performance

Do not reach for `memo`, `useMemo`, or `useCallback` by default. Measure first.
The React 19 compiler handles most of what hand-memoisation used to. Premature
memoisation adds allocation and hides bugs behind stale dependency arrays.
